{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content .agents/skills/architecture-decomposition/SKILL.md; Get-Content .agents/skills/antidote/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\r\nname: architecture-decomposition\r\ndescription: Use when planning a work item (answering its architectural-delta question) or at DevStg-Arch (breaking down a component scope), and in an architecture audit — name the mode classes, state each boundary's value domain, tabulate output against input dependencies, and apply A8's two descent rules, judged against the shared architecture-decomposition rubric.\r\nstacks: [any]\r\ndomains: [any]\r\nphases: [dev, gate]\r\ntags: [architecture, decomposition, interfaces, components, planning]\r\nscope: kit\r\n---\r\n**When to use.** Three moments: at `DevStg-Arch`, when a component scope is broken into sub-components; when planning a work item, to answer its architectural-delta question; and in an audit of a scope as it stands. *Why:* a boundary is worth its interface only when it hides a decision likely to change, and that is argued from the scope's states, domains and dependencies, not from the steps its processing runs through. The bar is the rubric at `docs/rubrics/architecture-decomposition.md`; this skill is the procedure that produces what the rubric judges, and it cites the rubric's anchors by id instead of restating them.\r\n\r\n**Procedure.**\r\n1. **Name the mode classes** (rubric A2). Record each one, with its transitions, as the scope's state flow in `docs/runtime-flows.md`.\r\n2. **State each boundary's value domain** (A3, A6). Record it as the form of the boundary's interface row in `docs/requirements/interfaces.toml`.\r\n3. **Tabulate output against input dependencies** (A4). Record the table beside the scope's flow in `docs/runtime-flows.md`, or, when planning, in the plan.\r\n4. **Apply A8's two descent rules.** Requirement descent for the scope's obligations; component descent after comparing the candidate cuts, keeping the scope whole among them, under A1, A4 and A7. Record the result in the existing scoped decomposition or review record (process-options.md \"(d2)\"); where the component layer keeps one, that is the component registry's partition record.\r\n\r\nEach output goes only to a home that already exists; the procedure never adds a file or a field to hold it.\r\n\r\n**When planning a work item.** Each step's result answers one clause of the delta question. A new mode class or transition is \"add a state\"; a new case in a domain is \"add a value\"; a new cell in the dependency table is \"a new input\"; a new boundary interface is \"cross a new boundary\". The plan writes its `DELTA:` line from those results, and carries a `within` change as rows of its own.\r\n\r\n**Below a component's boundary**, in its code, use the `deep-module-design` skill.\r\n---\r\nname: antidote\r\ndescription: Use when coding, reviewing, refactoring, auditing, committing fixes, or adding guards, validation, workarounds, or 20+ lines without deleting code — stop and find the root-cause fix instead of patching the symptom.\r\nstacks: [any]\r\ndomains: [any]\r\nphases: [dev]\r\ntags: [root-cause, refactoring, code-review, anti-pattern, vendored]\r\nscope: kit\r\n---\r\n\r\n> **Vendored** from [Avtr99/antidote](https://github.com/Avtr99/antidote)\r\n> `skills/antidote/SKILL.md`, MIT license, source commit\r\n> `8e0350e3d86df36852d56ad0a502376e24de870c` (2026-08-22, upstream version\r\n> 1.1.0; OI-58, WI-507). Content below is verbatim from upstream except this\r\n> note and the frontmatter above, rewritten to this kit's skill schema\r\n> (`skills/README.md`) — the original carried its own `name`/`description` and\r\n> a `license`/`metadata` block this kit's frontmatter contract has no field\r\n> for. This is the same consolidation principle stated at repo scale in\r\n> [`PROCESS.md` §3](../../PROCESS.md) (\"Consolidate, don't duplicate — the\r\n> 0→A→B rule\"): validate and implement once, at the boundary that owns the\r\n> behavior, never re-derived at each caller. The doctrine there governs\r\n> restructuring a codebase; this skill is its per-fix companion at the single\r\n> change in front of you.\r\n\r\n# Antidote\r\n\r\nAI agents often fix symptoms. A bug appears, and the agent adds a check for that one path. The check does not remove the bug. It only hides it on that path. The next path that uses the same data does not have the check. The bug comes back. After many patches, the code holds together with guards that no one remembers.\r\n\r\nBefore you write a fix, ask this: \"What is the smallest change that makes this fix unnecessary?\" Use a stricter type, delete a code path, or use a library. This work looks larger than a patch, but it costs less over time. A patch is cheap today and expensive tomorrow. A structural fix is the reverse.\r\n\r\n## Stop and rethink before you\r\n\r\n- Write a function that only checks, cleans, or coerces a value before the rest of the code uses it. Can a stricter type, schema, or interface make the bad shape impossible to build? Then nothing must check for it.\r\n- Add a defensive guard, such as a null check, an empty catch block, or a \"this should not happen\" branch. Ask why that state is reachable. Fix the cause, not the symptom.\r\n- Work around a library or framework behavior. Read the docs for the intended pattern first. A workaround breaks on the next upgrade. The intended pattern does not.\r\n- Duplicate logic in a new code path. Can both paths use one implementation?\r\n- Write 50 or more lines for a \"simple\" bug. You likely fix a symptom. Find the root cause.\r\n- Add a config flag for an edge case. The edge case may only exist because the design is wrong.\r\n\r\n## A library is one option, not the default answer\r\n\r\nThe goal is the smallest correct fix. Sometimes the fix is a library. Sometimes it is a type. Sometimes it is a deleted code path. Sometimes it is a few honest lines. Do not add a dependency by reflex.\r\n\r\nA library helps when the problem is hard and already solved: retries, caching, pagination, circuit breakers, state machines, config validation, and date parsing. Check what the project already uses before you add a new one.\r\n\r\nA library does not help when the custom code it replaces is only a few lines. Importing a package to avoid one conditional is its own kind of overcomplication. Now you carry its API, its versions, and its bugs. The line to watch is not \"custom code exists.\" It is \"custom code re-implements something hard that a good library already does well.\"\r\n\r\n## Two examples of the same fix\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# PATCH: clear the field on every write path; miss one, and the bug returns\r\ndef sanitize_order(order):\r\n    if order[\"kind\"] == \"digital\":\r\n        order[\"shipping_address\"] = None\r\n    return order\r\n\r\n# FIX: the variant has no such field, so nothing can set it\r\nclass DigitalOrder(BaseModel):\r\n    kind: Literal[\"digital\"]\r\n    download_url: str\r\n\r\nclass PhysicalOrder(BaseModel):\r\n    kind: Literal[\"physical\"]\r\n    shipping_address: Address\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# PATCH: every caller defends against the same bad shape\r\ndef process(data):\r\n    if data is None: return\r\n    if \"items\" not in data: return\r\n    for item in data.get(\"items\") or []:\r\n        if not item or \"name\" not in item: continue\r\n        ...\r\n\r\n# FIX: validate once at the boundary, then trust the type\r\nclass RequestData(BaseModel):\r\n    items: list[Item]\r\n\r\ndef process(data: RequestData):\r\n    for item in data.items:\r\n        ...  # item.name is guaranteed\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can use the same mechanism in any language. TypeScript, Zod, Go structs, Rust enums, Kotlin sealed classes, and Java records let you make an invalid state impossible. You do not need to detect it later.\r\n\r\n## Checklist before you write the fix\r\n\r\n1. State the root cause in one sentence. If you cannot, you do not understand it yet.\r\n2. Check the structure. Would a stricter type, a merged code path, or a deleted feature make this fix unnecessary?\r\n3. Check the library. Use a library only if the problem is hard and already solved. Skip it if a few lines will do.\r\n4. Check what to delete. What code does this fix let you remove? If nothing, you add complexity, not resolve it.\r\n\r\n## Red flags that you are patching, not fixing\r\n\r\n- The fix is 20 or more lines and deletes nothing.\r\n- It is a catch block that swallows an error or silently defaults.\r\n- It guards against a state that \"should not happen.\"\r\n- It re-implements something hard that a good library already does well.\r\n- It works around framework behavior instead of using the framework's pattern.\r\n- It is a new write path instead of reusing the existing one.\r\n\r\n## Audit mode\r\n\r\nWhen the user asks for an audit, do these steps:\r\n\r\n1. Scan for the patterns above. Record the file and line, the pattern, the risk, and the simpler option.\r\n2. Rank by impact. Start with small structural changes that remove large amounts of defensive code.\r\n3. Flag custom retry, cache, validation, or state-machine code. Replace it with a stricter type, structure, or library, whichever is smaller.\r\n4. Output a prioritized plan. Do not start fixing until the user approves it.\r\n\r\n## Fix mode\r\n\r\n1. State the root cause in one sentence.\r\n2. Propose the simplest fix that addresses it. If it is not simpler than what is there, it is the wrong fix.\r\n3. Check if a type change, a structure change, or a library removes the need for custom logic. Pick the smallest option. Do not default to either.\r\n4. Run tests before and after. If there are no tests, write a characterization test first.\r\n5. Delete whatever the fix makes unnecessary.\r\n6. Confirm that the bug is now structurally impossible, not just currently prevented.\r\n\r\n## The test\r\n\r\n> If a new developer adds a code path tomorrow without reading your fix, does the bug come back?\r\n\r\nIf the answer is yes, you wrote a patch. Go back and find the root cause.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'git show 8ddf8387b1fb7ce4aee486c8b361dcad0db8e332:docs/work/active/wi-996/WI-996-strict-red-mark-check.md'","aggregated_output":"+++\nid = \"WI-996\"\ntitle = \"A removed red mark is proven red by the lane's last review record, no red mark lands, and `[bars] red_mark` declares the mark\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-156\"]\nspecref = \"docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md#13-review-only-a-green-tip-review-test-cases-in-stages\"\nneeds = [\"WI-995\", \"OI-125\"]\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nplanmode = \"single\"\npriority = 5\n+++\n\n## Context\n\nP8's three rows (WI-992's filing, the review-churn plan's item 13): row 1 is WI-994, row 2 WI-995 and row 3 WI-996; \"row N\" below names them so.\n\n**What the plan rules.** Review-churn item 13 (ruled as a whole in\nrevision 35; mechanism from revisions 20 and 23) proves a test's red-before\nwith a strict expected-failure mark. The tests-first commit lands each new\ntest under the mark. Its review-bar attestation records the bar it passed,\nso that green bar is the red-before: a strictly marked test the bar runs\npasses it only by failing at an assertion, and \"a skip is a skip, never\nevidence\". The code commit removes the marks. \"A\ncommit-versus-parent check confirms that each removed mark was present in\nthe parent, that the parent carries a green attestation of the review bar,\nand that the test now passes under the same bar. That is a static check,\nlight enough for ruling 6's commit-bar command; the bar itself is the\nexecution.\" The Python reference is\n`@pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True, raises=(AssertionError, pytest.fail.Exception))`.\nOther stacks supply their equivalent, restricted to assertion failures. The\nowner accepted `[bars] red_mark` as a required key, a forced adopter\nmigration stated in a RESYNC entry (OI-120 Q2 (a), 2026-11-02).\n\n**Where `[bars]` lives (re-measured).** `[bars]` is not in\n`docs/process.toml`. Its sections are `attestation`, `policies`, `checks`,\n`adjudicator`, `reviewer`, `coordinator` and `build`\n(`grep -n \"^\\[\" docs/process.toml`). The section is in `docs/stack.ini`\n(line 1360) and in `project-trajectory/stack.ini.template` (line 254). Each\nholds exactly one key, `review` (`grep -n \"^\\[bars\\]\" -A8` on both files).\nItem 10's fold of `stack.ini` into `process.toml` is not queued. So this row\nadds the key where the section lives now, and the fold moves the whole\nsection later. `[bars]` has one parse site,\n`integrate._review_bar_command` (`grep -rn '\"bars\"'\nproject-trajectory/scripts` returns only `integrate.py:3001`). Its contract\ntext is IF-022's, in `docs/stack.ini`'s header (lines 11 to 26), and\n`docs/interface-reference.md` is regenerated from it.\n\n**What exists today (re-measured).**\n\n- **Marks in this repository: none.** The whitespace-stripped Python\n  reference appears 0 times across the 306 files under `tests/` (269\n  `test_*.py`), checked with a `pathlib` count run under the repository's\n  `.venv` with `-I`. The one `xfail(strict=True` text under `tests/` is\n  `tests/test_prompts.py:308`. It is a clause literal split across two\n  string literals, pinning WI-955's skill text, so it is not a mark.\n- **The order is text only.** WI-955 put the tests-first order and the\n  mark into the coordinator-cycle skill\n  (`project-trajectory/skills/coordinator-cycle/SKILL.md:194-205`, with the\n  `.claude` and `.agents` copies). It says \"committed alone before the\n  code\". It does not say that the commit goes through `attest`, and nothing\n  checks the order.\n- **Callers of `attest`.**\n  - The coordinator calls it by hand: `integrate.py attest --tests <files>`\n    (recipes §6).\n  - The agent loop calls it in two places, both with an empty test set:\n    `agent_loop.py:2498` (`attest_build`) and `:3384` (the draw).\n  - The attestation records only a digest of the test set (`tests=`,\n    `kverdict.tests_digest`), not the list.\n- **Lane-local history is already read.** `kverdict._last_review_green(root,\n  branch, base)` already finds the newest verified `review` attestation in\n  `base..branch` (`red_rounds` reads it). `integrate._lane_base` names the\n  lane's base, with a fail-closed refusal.\n- **Fixtures that declare `[bars]`.**\n  - `tests/conftest.py` `declare_review_bar` is called five times from\n    `test_agent_loop_review.py` (twice), `test_agent_loop_review_gate.py`\n    and `test_agent_loop_worker.py`.\n  - `tests/test_integrate_attest.py` writes the section inline (its refusal\n    table, lines 225 to 227, and line 258).\n  - `tests/test_integrate_station.py:224` writes it inline.\n  - `tests/test_check_smoke_budget.py:490` reads `[bars] review` and is\n    unaffected.\n- **`check_test_first.py` is untouched.** It is the warn-only `test-first`\n  step (SR-217, IF-198). It reads the approval order of test-case rows\n  against the code's landing from trunk history. That is a different\n  question from a test's red-before, and this row does not change it (see\n  \"Not in this row\").\n\n**The anchor.** SR-156 is the anchor (\"a finished branch reaches the\nintegration branch only through the serial seam with the declared bar green\non the composed tree\"). Both arms extend the one attestation producer that\nLLR-207 states under SR-156. A strictly marked test passes the bar while it\nis red, so a landing tree holding a mark is not green in SR-156's sense.\nNeither SR-156 nor LLR-207 states the red-before obligation today. The\nchange set's spine authoring at intent decides between a new LLR under\nSR-156 beside LLR-207 and a labelled derived SR. This row does not write\nrequirement text.\n\n## Scope\n\n`integrate.py attest` gains the strict-mark check: a static comparison,\nbefore the bar runs, of the lane's staged tree against the tree of the\nlane's last review attestation. Each test file whose count of the declared\nred mark rises must be in the round's test set. Each test file whose count\nfalls must also be in that set, and the last review attestation must be\nover the same test set. The refresh refuses any landing tree whose tests\nroot still holds the mark. The mark is declared once, as `[bars] red_mark`\nbeside `[bars] review`. The key is required: if it is absent or empty,\n`attest` and the refresh both refuse and name it. There is no default.\nThird of P8's three rows (WI-992, OI-120 Q2 (a)). The coordinator\nsubstitutes `WI-995` with row 2's filed id.\n\n## The rule (S3: closed)\n\n**The declaration.** `[bars] red_mark` is read with `[bars] review` through\none parse of `docs/stack.ini`. It is read from the same tree as\n`[bars] review`: the lane's staged checkout at `attest`, and the tree the\nrefresh bars at the refresh. A value that is non-empty after stripping is\nthe mark's text. Every other state refuses, naming `docs/stack.ini\n[bars] red_mark` and the cause:\n\n- the file is absent;\n- the file does not parse;\n- the section is absent;\n- the key is absent;\n- the value is empty.\n\nThe refusal comes before any bar runs. There is no default and no fallback\nto another key. The test files are the paths under `[paths] tests`,\nresolved as the harness resolves it (one home).\n\n**Counting.** A file's mark count is the number of non-overlapping\noccurrences of the declared text in the file's bytes. ASCII whitespace is\nremoved from both the file and the text before counting, so a formatter\nthat wraps the call's arguments still counts. Bytes are counted, never\ndecoded, so no file is undecodable.\n\n**The attest arms.** The reference is the newest commit in the lane's own\nrange (`_lane_base`..HEAD) that carries a verified `review` attestation. If\nno commit in the range carries one, the reference is the lane base, which\ncarries no review record. The compared paths are the test files that\ndiffer between the reference's tree and the staged tree. A rename counts\nas a deletion plus an addition. Write m0 for a file's count at the\nreference and m1 for its count in the staged tree; an absent file counts 0.\nT is the round's test set, the set the new attestation's `tests=` digest\ncovers. \"T names P\" means that an entry of T, normalized as `tests_digest`\nnormalizes (a backslash to `/`, a leading `./` dropped), equals P. A node\nid (`P::name`) or a directory does not name P.\n\n| State of test file P | Ruling |\n|---|---|\n| m1 = m0 (0 = 0 included) | not judged |\n| m1 > m0: marks added, or a new file that carries marks | passes when T names P. Otherwise refused, naming P: the bar would not run its marked tests, so their red is unproven |\n| m1 < m0, and P is present in the staged tree: marks removed | passes when the reference carries a verified `review` attestation whose tests digest equals T's, and T names P. Otherwise refused, naming P and which condition failed: no review record stands for the marked tree, the record is for another test set, or the set does not name P |\n| m1 < m0, and P is absent from the staged tree (deleted or renamed away) | not judged here: the test is gone, its TC loses its tagged file, and row 2's partial-set refusal names that |\n| git cannot read the range, the reference's tree or the staged tree | refused, naming the read: an unread delta is not an empty one |\n\nRemoving a mark therefore needs three things:\n\n- the mark was present at the last tree the lane attested;\n- that tree's green bar ran the marked file under the strict mark, which is\n  the red-before of each marked test it did not skip;\n- the new bar runs the file unmarked over the same test set, which is the\n  green-after.\n\nA plain commit between two attestations is judged at the next attestation,\nbecause the reference is the last attested tree, not HEAD.\n\n**The landing arm.** The refresh, on the tree it bars and before the bar\nruns, refuses when any file under the tests root has a mark count above 0.\nIt names each file and its count. This applies to both refresh bars\n(`landing` and `no-bar`). A tree with no marks passes. Unreadable files\nrefuse as above. Marks are therefore never on trunk. That keeps the attest\narms' compared paths the lane's own, even after a merge-refresh brings\ntrunk in.\n\n**What the bar already does.** A mark left on a test that now passes is the\nbar's to refuse: an unexpected pass under a strict mark fails it. An error\nthat is not an assertion failure also fails it. The check does not judge\neither case.\n\n**A marked test the bar skips.** A skip exits 0, so the bar stays green and\nthe strict mark judges nothing. The record is then not that test's\nred-before: a skip is a skip, never evidence (item 13). The check cannot see\nthis state. `attest` reads the bar's exit code, and reading each test's\noutcome is the evidence record revision 20 replaced (\"no separate evidence\nrecord, scratch tree or junit parsing\"). So the procedure closes it: a test\nthe bar skips takes no mark. It has no red-before on that machine, and the\nlane names it with its disposition in the decisions record, the route the\nevidence step already gives a test that fits no class. Its green-after is\nthe bar wherever the bar runs it. A skipped test left marked is not refused;\nthe reviewers judge it with the test's adequacy.\n\n## Not in this row\n\n- **Tagging tests, and the reader of the tags:** row 1.\n- **Deriving the review test set, and refusing an empty or partial set or a\n  code row that names no SR:** row 2. After row 2, T is that derived set.\n- **Requiring a new test to carry a mark.** A characterization test is\n  unmarked, and its adequacy witness is judged by the reviewers. In
